   213|<section class="chapter" id="chapter-5"><h3>Chapter 5: The Spark Ignites</h3><img alt="Chapter 5: The Spark Ignites" class="chapter-image" src="data:image/png;base64,... [truncated]
   214|<p>The summer before tenth grade, Bob's father announced that it was time for his son to learn what work actually meant. Not the abstract work of school assignments, not the project work of the garage, but the clock-punching, customer-serving, money-earning work that occupied the majority of adult life. He had arranged a job for Bob at Hendricks TV &amp; Electronics, a small repair shop that smelled of solder flux and ozone and the particular mustiness of vacuum tubes past their prime. Mr. Hendricks was a bald man with perpetually cigarette-stained fingers and a habit of talking to himself while he worked. He took one look at the fifteen-year-old standing in his doorway and said, "Can you solder?"—"Yes, sir."—"Show me."</p>
   215|<p>Bob spent the next ten minutes demonstrating what he knew—how to tin an iron, how to heat the joint rather than the solder, how to create that bright, concave fillet that meant a good connection. Mr. Hendricks watched without comment, then handed him a basket of vacuum tubes. "Test these. The checker is in the back. Write down which ones are bad. When you're done, sweep the floor. You're hired. After that, we'll see about teaching you something."</p>
   216|<p>The job paid $1.25 an hour, minimum wage, but what it offered in education was worth far more. Bob spent that summer and the two that followed driving around town doing pickup and delivery with an older worker and learning the applied electronics that would form the foundation of his career. He learned to read schematic diagrams, those intricate maps of electron flow that turned abstract symbols into working circuits. He learned to trace signals through televisions and radios, to identify failure points by symptom, to repair rather than replace because customers couldn't afford new sets.</p>
   217|<h4>The First Car</h4>
   218|<p>Bob's first car was a Chrysler Valiant. It was blue, and had a mind of its own. The heater only worked when it wanted to, which in Michigan meant I spent most of the winter driving with my windows cracked to keep the windshield from fogging up. The manual gear shift had a mind of its own, sometimes finding third when he was reaching for first, sometimes refusing to find any gear at all until I'd pumped the clutch three times.</p>
   219|<p>Bob's parents bought it with the arrangement that Bob would pay them back. Bob earned the money to buy it, working weekends at a local TV and radio repair shop. It represented something more than transportation—it represented independence, achievement, the first tangible evidence that Bob could provide for himself. Bob remembered driving to the lake with his friends, the wind in their hair, the radio blasting whatever station they could pick up. They had no destination in the strictest sense—they just drove. They followed roads they'd never taken, discovered places they'd never known existed, pulled over at random diners to eat french fries and just lived life.</p>
